  :adr/body "# 40 bots that no fetch could see\n\n`/api/bots-status` is published by ONE process on this workstation and stored\nunder ONE KV key. Before today that publisher enumerated exactly one bot\npopulation -- the launchd `com.gftd.*` Claude loops, 29 of them in the live\ndocument. Measured 2026-08-31, `~/.hermes/cron/jobs.json` holds **40 active\nHermes cron residents** on the same machine, and none of them appeared\nanywhere a fetch could reach: not on itonami.cloud/bots/, not in the\ndocument, not at any endpoint.\n\ncloud-itonami ADR-0049 built that page because \"telemetry existed only in\nlocal ledgers on the operator workstation; no fetch could answer *did the\nloop run?*\". That sentence was true of forty more bots at the time it was\nwritten, and it stayed true. It has already cost something once: the\n`hermes-hyakka-bots` README records two itonami scouts left pointing at the\nfleet model for a day after a switch -- one spending 57 minutes and 1.86M\ninput tokens overnight -- with nothing broken and nothing reported.\n\n## The decision\n\n`scripts/loop-status-publish.cljs` publishes BOTH populations in the SAME\n`cloud.itonami.bots-status.v1` document: `loops` unchanged, plus a new\n`residents` block read from `~/.hermes/cron/jobs.json`.\n\n**One publisher, because there is one key.** A second publisher writing\n`current` would silently overwrite the first, and both would receive 204.\nThat is the failure mode this workspace keeps rediscovering -- an operation\nreporting success while losing data -- so the shape is refused rather than\nguarded.\n\n**A cron resident is not scored like a loop.** A loop promises an interval\nand is late outside 2x it. A resident promises the next fire time its own\nscheduler computed. The derivation lives in\n`cloud-itonami.bots-status/resident-status` and its gates are ordered so a\nresident cannot reach :ok by owning a future `next_run`: disabled or paused\n-> :held, failure streak or failing status -> :failed, never run or no next\nfire -> :unmeasured, past `next_run` + 15 min -> :stale.\n\n**Unreadable is not empty.** Five distinct failures can stop the reader\n(`hermes-home-absent`, `jobs-file-absent`, `jobs-file-unreadable`,\n`jobs-file-invalid-json`, `jobs-file-shape-unexpected`) and every one returns\nits own name in `residents.why` with NO `jobs` key. A reader that returned\n`{:jobs []}` on error would report \"this operation runs no residents\", which\nis the answer that must never come from a failure -- and the `--self-test`\ngoes red in exactly that way when the reader is broken that way, which is how\nit was checked.\n\n## What does NOT leave this machine\n\nThree fields on a Hermes job are local facts, and the page they would reach\nis public:\n\n| field | why it stays |\n|---|---|\n| `workdir` | an absolute path under the operator's home |\n| `prompt` | the agent's instructions |\n| `last_error` | the failed script's raw **stdout** |\n\nThe third was found by publishing it. The first real document carried a\nresident's `at://` record URI and the post body that went with it, inside\nwhat read like an innocuous error string. The publisher now sends\n`has_error` -- a boolean -- so the page can say \"an error is recorded on the\nworkstation\" without republishing arbitrary process output.\n\n## Measured\n\n    nbb --classpath \".:scripts/nbb_compat\" scripts/loop-status-publish.cljs --self-test\n      12 checks, both directions. Collapsing the reader's error branches to\n      {:jobs []} turns two of them red.\n    nbb --classpath \".:scripts/nbb_compat\" scripts/loop-status-publish.cljs --dry-run\n      29 loops + 40 residents = 23,880 bytes (the route's cap is 65,536).\n      No home-directory path and no prompt text anywhere in the document.\n\nFeeding that document to the page's own projection\n(`cloud-itonami.site.bots-runtime/residents-model`) returns 40 rows, 39 :ok\nand 1 :failed -- `bots-aozora-pulse`, which had been failing where nothing\noutside this machine could see it.\n\n## What this is not\n\nIt is not a second bots design. The vocabulary, the never-green rule and the\nrow presentation are cloud-itonami-app's `bot-slo`, mirrored once in\n`cloud-itonami.bots-status` and now REQUIRED by the page projection rather\nthan restated a third time.\n\nIt is not a claim that the residents are healthy. It is the first\narrangement in which that question has an address."}]
